A||||20||Standard||poly vs. rubber tranny mounts||||1||
Z||000000||Standard||09-17-2001||12:28 AM||||Well, my front tranny mount is toast so it's time for replacement.  I would like to go with C. West's poly tranny mounts, but at 65 bucks each vs. about 50 bucks total for rubber mounts I'm wondering if it's really worth it.  right now the wallet is leaning towards the rubber, but I don't want to have to replace it again in a couple of months.  Any thoughts/suggestions?<P>oh yeah.. this is for the quad.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||09-17-2001||12:53 AM||||Personal opinion, I'd go with the plain rubber (maybe a poly dogbone). I don't think poly motor mounts would make much difference.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||09-17-2001||01:39 AM||||That's what i'm thinking right now.. also, no dogbone here.  Uses one of V8 Archies big torque struts mounted to the cradle.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||09-17-2001||02:48 AM||||Rubber mounts were well under $10 a piece at Autozone, and they actually fit.  <BR>I tried Sealed Power mounts from Discount Auto, and the studs just barely (but bad enough) didn't line up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||09-17-2001||09:16 AM||||Standard,<P>Either you are really rough on the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> or those <B>BRAND NEW</B> transaxle mounts are junk.<P>I have a <B>BRAND NEW</B> transaxle mount from The Fiero Store that the metal part that bolts to the transaxle bracket came off the rubber.  I hadn't even bolted it on to a car yet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Of course I purchased WELL in advance so I can't return it.<P>Now if this is an example of what is out there (remanufactured rubber mounts), what will we do for transaxle mounts in the future (in your case ... the future is NOW!)?<P>I have thought real hard about buying the mounts from C. West but after the childish behavior in another thread ... I don't think I want to do business with that kind of people.<P>The above paragraph is my opinion ... your results may vary ... not valid in the state of California ... yada, yada, yada.<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||09-17-2001||09:20 AM||||I made my own Poly mounts for my 3400.  The rubber sags under the pressure and torque.  I feel the engine vibrating now.  It isn't that bad.  I feel a lot more secure having lots more torque in the car using the poly. Rubber=mushy.  I'm sure the engine's torque is now put right to the axles instead of twisting the whole engine/trans.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||09-17-2001||12:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>Standard,<P>Either you are really rough on the car   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> or those [b]BRAND NEW</B> transaxle mounts are junk.<P>Paul[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think it's a combo of the two  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Since I don't have the finances right now I think I'm gonna stick with rubber.<P>I'm with ya on the childish behavior, but so long as the product is good and I don't have to deal with them I'll probably buy it in the near future, unless someone can point me to another maker of poly trans mounts..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||HarryG||09-17-2001||12:53 PM||||Joe Torma:<P>Did you use off-the-shelf poly bushings or make your own? ||||reg||5
Z||000008||c m west||09-17-2001||12:57 PM||||granted the polyurathane mounts are highly resistant and durable in the confines near the exhaust heat and hold up well over time, it would not be very viable or practicle with a Quad Four unless you would'nt mind the vibration at idle. I would say the best bet at this time would be to go with known good stock mounts such as the type from the Fiero Store.<P> Just from past experience the vibration from a 4 cyl. with our polyurathane mounts is nearly enough to rattle your fillings out.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||09-17-2001||01:11 PM||||I don't think i'd mind the extra vibrations.. actually, I'm thinking about going with just a poly front tranny mount and keeping the rest rubber, the front seems to take the most beating.  The rear tranny mount looks fine, so does the engine mount.<P>Chris, how long would it take to get a mount up here to minnesota??||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jimmy||09-17-2001||02:05 PM||||Standard I think Darel Morris makes poly mounts.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||09-17-2001||02:08 PM||||Really?  how can i get in touch with him?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Joe Torma||09-17-2001||02:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HarryG:<BR><B>Joe Torma:<P>Did you use off-the-shelf poly bushings or make your own? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Half and half.  I used Camaro trans mounts.  I'll post pics later.  They are very strong and have a safety interlock.<P>My front motor mount is different than all of yours, but I would recommend replacing all mounts with poly if you are going to do it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||c m west||09-17-2001||09:33 PM||||I can have them there to you in 2 days, I think replacing just the front mount would'nt be a bad idea at all, not only does years of load from the engine torquing get to it, but so does the exhaust.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000014||pherder||09-17-2001||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>I think replacing just the front mount would'nt be a bad idea at all, not only does years of load from the engine torquing get to it, but so does the exhaust.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B>WRONG!!!</B><P>First of all, the car has a Quad 4 engine with the exhaust at the rear <B>ONLY!</B> So there isn't an exhaust baking problem with the front transaxle mount.<P>Second, the mounts (from The Fiero Store) were brand new &lt; 500 miles when I sold the car to Standard.  So age isn't a problem either.<P>The problem is the mounts were "remanufactured", i.e. new rubber was molded into the steel mounts and I bet the top steel mount did not hold to the rubber.  I have one here in my shop that did exactly the same thing.  <P>No doubt that a set of poly mounts would solve the problem but I for one would not buy from a company that posts the kind of crap I have seen in The Mall section under the thread  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/004817.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/004817.html</A> <P>Paul||||reg||5
Z||000015||Standard||09-17-2001||10:46 PM||||ya beat me to it paul  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>"I bet the top steel mount did not hold to the rubber"<P>That feels like what happened.. we'll find out in about 30 minutes when I go out to replace it.<P>Ended up stopping by napa on my way to work.. they had a front tranny mount in stock for 12 bucks.  <I>looks</I> like a pretty good unit and for only 12 bucks I won't complain if they only last a month.<P>No offense to you, eric, but if darrell does make them I'd rather buy from him and support the local Fiero guy.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Standard||09-18-2001||12:39 AM||||ok, just got the front mount off.<P>The rubber bushing is almost completly torn off the bottom steel plate that bolts to the cradle.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  thank god for u-joint extensions.  Took some serious contorting to get at that top bolt.<P>And being able to get a better look at the rear tranny mount, i can see it's starting to go, too.  d'oh..  at least the passenger side engine mount it holding up fine. <P>oh yeah.. it didn't take me two hours to do this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  actually didn't get to start till about an hour ago.  I'm not slow, I just take my time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Standard||09-18-2001||02:38 AM||||Alright, she's all buttoned up and ready to go.<P>The rear mount is worse than I thought, it's tearing off the metal plate just like the front one.  I put a hose clamp around that tab to (hopefully) keep it from moving too much.  I doubt the clamp will last very long.. <P>I coulda had the whole thing done in less than an hour if I didn't take so many breaks.  Pretty easy job.  The engine is much more solid now.  Hopefully it'll stay that way for a little while.  But I'm definatly going to go with poly mounts pretty soon.. just seems to me that the design of these rubber mounts downright sucks.  And I like the idea of having a built-in 180 hp back massager  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||09-18-2001||04:01 AM||||Does the 4 cyl vibrate that much more than the V6?  Standard wants rubber engine bushings, Haze wants rubber dogbone bushings, etc.  I have a brand new ultimate dogbone on my 2.8 and don't feel any noticable vibration.  (Of course, my motor mounts could be a mass of Jello at this point.)  Why does the 2.8 vibrate less?  Design of the motor or design of the car?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Leper||09-18-2001||04:23 AM||||As a rule of thumb, the more cylinders you have firing in an engine the smoother it will run. A 2.8 does run smoother than an iron duke.||||reg||1
Z||000020||c m west||09-18-2001||01:00 PM||||when I was reffering to earlier to the wear of the mount, I was reffering to its most common application which is to a 2.8 and similar engines. I have never worked with a quad 4 so please do not bite my head off, if you do not want to use our, mounts I won't make you||||reg||1
